# Tor Controller

A terminal-based Tor connection manager for Windows. Start, stop, and monitor Tor from a single interactive menu.

## Requirements

- **Node.js v14+** (bundled in `vendor/nodejs/`)
- **Tor** (bundled in `vendor/tor-bundle/`)

### Download Links (if vendor files are missing)

| Component | URL |
|-----------|-----|
| Node.js v14.16.1 (win-x86) | https://nodejs.org/dist/v14.16.1/node-v14.16.1-win-x86.zip |
| Tor 0.4.5.7 (win32) | https://www.torproject.org/dist/torbrowser/10.0.15/tor-win32-0.4.5.7.zip |

## Usage

**Interactive mode:**
```
run.bat
```
or
```
vendor\nodejs\node.exe run.js
```

**CLI mode:**
```
vendor\nodejs\node.exe run.js 1    # Start Tor
vendor\nodejs\node.exe run.js 7    # Show exit IP
vendor\nodejs\node.exe run.js q    # Quit
```

## Menu Options

| Key | Feature | Description |
|-----|---------|-------------|
| `1` | **Start TOR** | Launches the Tor process and connects to the network. Automatically fetches and displays your exit IP on successful connection. |
| `2` | **Log Info** | Dumps Tor connection log information via the control port. Useful for debugging connection issues. |
| `3` | **Stop TOR** | Gracefully shuts down the running Tor process. |
| `4` | **Debug** | Enables Tor debug logging to see detailed network activity. |
| `5` | **New Identity** | Requests a new Tor circuit, giving you a fresh exit IP and path through the network. |
| `6` | **Restart** | Stops the current Tor instance and immediately starts a new one. |
| `7` | **Exit IP** | Queries `api.ipify.org` through Tor's SOCKS5 proxy to display your current public exit IP address. |
| `8` | **Circuit Path** | Displays all active Tor circuits with their relay path: Entry -> Hops -> Exit. Shows circuit ID, state, and purpose. |
| `9` | **Network Stats** | Shows a dashboard with uptime, bandwidth rate/burst, total data transferred, network status, active circuits, and exit IP. |

## Architecture

```
run.js          Main application (menu, actions, SOCKS5 proxy helper)
torrc           Tor configuration (SocksPort 9050, ControlPort 9051)
run.bat         Windows batch launcher
vendor/
  nodejs/       Bundled Node.js runtime
  tor-bundle/   Bundled Tor binary + DLLs
```

## How It Works

- **Tor process** is spawned as a child process and tracked in memory
- **Control port** (9051) is used via the `tor-control` library to send signals (NEWNYM, HALT, DUMP, DEBUG) and query info (circuit-status, bandwidth, uptime)
- **SOCKS5 proxy** (9050) is used to make HTTP requests through Tor for exit IP detection -- implemented using Node.js `net` module with no external dependencies
- **Graceful shutdown** kills the Tor process on Ctrl+C or `Q` key

## Notes

- Ports 9050 (SOCKS) and 9051 (Control) must be free before starting Tor
- If Tor is already running from another application, stop it first or use option 3
- The GeoIP path warnings on startup are non-fatal and can be ignored
- Vendor binaries are excluded from git (see `.gitignore`)
